  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nActive weather is expected for much of the upcoming week with \nmultiple upper-level disturbances pushing through the region. Monday \nfeatures the strongest disturbance expected for the week as a stout \nupper-level trough digs across Montana with a 100kt jet along the \nsoutheastern portion. The jet will move eastward with the upper-\nlevel low, placing the CWA within the right entrance region of the \nupper-level jet for much of the day Monday. Enhanced, dynamically-\ndriven ascent will provide much needed forcing for showers and \nthunderstorms Monday morning and afternoon. Multiple 500mb vorticity \nmaxima will eject across the area as the upper-level low moves \neastward, favoring addition forcing across much of the region. The \n700mb low is expected to be displaced to the north across the \nsouthern Saskatchewan Province. While this low will be displaced to \nthe north, it looks to be quite strong, with an associated jet \naround 50 to 60kts over the Northern Plains. For Southeast Wyoming \nand Western Nebraska, the low will lead to strong, westerly flow \nacross the CWA around 40 to 45kts Monday morning. If you have lived \nin this area for a while now, you may know where this is heading... \nThe surface low with this system will dive southeastward from the \nBlack Hills towards central Nebraska, with a strong, trailing cold \nfront. This low progression is favorable for strong, west to east \nsurface pressure gradients to develop across western portions of the \narea and across the Laramie Range. The surface pressure gradient \nacross the Laramie Range peaks around 2 to 3mb Monday morning. GFS \nOmega field suggest modest to strong downward motion along the \nLaramie Range and just east. With all these factors considered, \nMonday morning will likely see very strong winds across much of the \nregion, though a High Wind Warning is currently not anticipated, but \nthis may change as this event moves into the short term. In-house \nrandom forest model guidance is giving around a 30 to 40% chance for \nhigh winds at Arlington, Rawlins, Bordeaux, Coleman, and northern \nCarbon County. Therefore, high wind criteria winds cannot be \ncompletely ruled out at this time and this setup will need to be \nmonitored closely as Monday morning approaches.\n\nIn addition to the potential for strong to near-high wind criteria \nwinds, this system will bring a strong cold front to the region, \ndropping temperatures from the low-70s to upper-80s on Monday into \nthe upper-60s to mid-70s on Tuesday. Most locations east of the \nLaramie Range will be around 10 to 12 degrees cooler on Tuesday. \nAdditionally, isolated to scattered showers will be possible Monday \nwith the front moving through, resulting in low-level forcing for \nascent. Dewpoints in the 50s and 60s east of the Laramie Range on \nMonday will favor precipitation development with the front, before \ndewpoints tank into the 30s and 40s behind the front. With the \nfrontal passage timing, precipitation will be largely confined to \nthe I-80s corridor across the region. The long range models \ncurrently suggest that some CAPE may be present across the region \nMonday morning and afternoon, so an isolated storm or two cannot be \nruled out especially across the southern Panhandle.\n\nAfter the cold front Monday, temperatures will remain quite nice \nthrough the remainder of the week, likely staying in the 60s and 70s \nwith a couple of low-80s possible Wednesday. Daily precipitation \nchances are expected as the upper-level flow remains more dynamic \nthan it has been in the past several weeks.",
